    "evidence_text": "Method\tIn this section, we describe our dataset as well as our tag recommendation approaches we propose to annotate e-books.\n\nDataset\tOur dataset contains two sources of data, one to generate tag recommendations and another one to evaluate tag recommendations.\n\nHGV GmbH has collected all data sources and we provide the dataset statistics in Table TABREF3 .\n\nData used to generate recommendations.\n\nRowohlt, Droemer, Fischer and Neopubli.\n\nApart from the search terms, this data contains the e-books' ISBNs, titles and description texts.\n\nData used to evaluate recommendations.\n\nFor evaluation, we use a third set of e-book annotations, namely Amazon review keywords.\n\ntags in the dataset, which is a common strategy for tag recommendations  .\n\nSimilarity-based approaches.\n\nand INLINEFORM5 ).\n\nWe again use a round-robin strategy to combine both data sources (= INLINEFORM3 and INLINEFORM4 ).\n\nHybrid approaches.\n\nWe use the previously mentioned cross-source algorithm  to construct four hybrid recommendation approaches.\n\n(ii) which use the description text.\n\nRecommendation accuracy.\n\nWe use Normalized Discounted\n\nWe then train a Doc2Vec  model on the content.\n\nFinally, we use the cosine similarity measure to calculate how semantically similar\n\nthese two lists are.\n\nSemantic similarity.\n\nFigure FIGREF16 illustrates the results of our proposed semantic similarity measure.\n\nHowever, by looking at\n\nwe show that they still offer helpful annotations concerning recommendation diversity as well as our novel semantic similarity metric.\n\nFuture work.\n\nWith this, we aim to demonstrate the usefulness of the proposed approach in a similar domain and to enhance the reproducibility of our results by using an open dataset.\n\nMoreover, we plan to evaluate our tag recommendation approaches in a study with domain users.\n\nAcknowledgments.\n\nCOMET is managed by the Austrian Research Promotion Agency (FFG).",
